er this seems  like you've got a place to talk about  how much I love the amount of power that  is put in the hands of what would  generally be considered elderly  characters usually when you've got older  people with power its power that they've  had for a long time and they're more  sort of monuments to what they used to  be and they're there for their wisdom I  don't know no no these guys have  raw pure power Tywin's probably the best  example but you also have the Lord  Commander of the Night Watch even Davos  The Onion Knight to a certain extent cuz  all these guys have inherent gravitas  and it's just wonderful to see it use so  effectively on the flip side of that  it's also really interesting to see the  characters who are losing their grips on  power a little bit Searcy is probably  the best example of this she's been on a  downward slope ever since the end of  season 1 when she realized that she is  not gonna be able to control Joffrey now  she's being outmaneuvered by his new  fiancee I didn't want to say though that  I think her drinking is being emphasized  a little bit too much no we get it  without her having a thing a wine in her  hand all the time also on the subject of  power loss is Joffrey himself he what  he's lost is not so much his power but  he certainly lost his confidence he's  realized at this point how precarious  his throne actually is which is  interesting it adds a new dynamic to him  he's still you still absolutely hate  this guy and you should but he's still  got a bit of dimension  Daenerys continues to be a bit of a  troublesome character for me she's  basically got two sides to her there's  the emerging leader who's got a real  sense of strength and then there's the  young almost girl who just is way in  over her head they're not blended  together very well they don't seem like  two aspects of the same person it feels  more like they flip her back and forth  between those two modes depending on  what any given scene calls for the 